course: religion-ethics
lesson: 27
title: Sunday school, youth groups and charity
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Church is not only a Sunday service: there are Sunday school classes for children, youth groups for older ones, and camps and holiday clubs full of games, stories and adventures.
explain: In these groups children learn Bible stories, pray, sing, make friends and are free to ask honest questions about faith.
explain: Jesus told a story about the sheep and the goats: whatever people do for the hungry, the stranger, the sick and the prisoner, they do for him (Matthew 25).
explain: That is why so many Christians run food banks, give to charities such as Christian Aid, CAFOD, Tearfund, the Salvation Army and Catholic Charities, and volunteer their time.
explain: For a child today it can be small and real: bringing a tin to the food bank, planning to give part of pocket money, or visiting a lonely neighbour with a grown-up.
fact: Sunday school is classes for children at church to learn Bible stories, pray, sing and make things.
fact: Youth groups are for older children and teenagers to meet, have fun, ask questions and grow in faith.
fact: Churches run camps and holiday clubs with games, stories and outdoor adventures.
fact: Charity means giving help, time or money to people in need, out of love.
fact: Christians believe that serving others is serving Jesus.
fact: In the story of the sheep and the goats, Jesus said that what we do for the hungry, the stranger, the sick and the prisoner we do for him; he called these people «the least of these».
fact: The story of the sheep and the goats is in Matthew chapter 25.
fact: Food banks, often run by churches, give food to families who cannot afford enough.
fact: Christian Aid, CAFOD, Tearfund, the Salvation Army and Catholic Charities help people in poverty and disaster.
fact: CAFOD is the charity of the Catholic Church in England and Wales.
fact: Tithing is the old practice of giving a tenth of one's income to God's work.
fact: Tithing teaches that giving can be planned, not only done now and then.
fact: Volunteering means giving time freely, for example visiting lonely people or cleaning up the area.
fact: Christians believe time can be a gift as precious as money.
exercise: What happens at Sunday school? | Children learn Bible stories, pray, sing and make things | Children sit school exams in maths and spelling | Grown-ups vote for a new church leader | Children are baptised one by one
exercise: Who are youth groups mainly for? | Older children and teenagers | Babies and their parents | Only church leaders | Only older people living alone
exercise: Why are honest questions welcome at youth group? | Faith includes friendship and honest questions | Questions are banned everywhere else, so youth group is the only place | Leaders need fresh ideas for the Sunday notices and the newsletter | Only questions about football and other sports are allowed there
exercise: What do church holiday clubs usually offer? | Games, stories and outdoor adventures | Extra homework to get ready for the next term at school | Jobs that pay children money | Lessons in how to run a shop
exercise: What is charity? | Giving help, time or money to those in need, out of love | Asking other people to give you their money | Buying presents only for your own family | Paying the bus fare for your own trip
exercise: Why do Christians believe serving others matters so much? | They believe serving others is serving Jesus | They believe it makes them famous and admired in their town | They believe it is only a school rule | They believe God pays them back in money
exercise: In the story of the sheep and the goats, who does Jesus say we help when we help the hungry? | Jesus himself | Only the farmer | Only the king's soldiers | Only our own family
exercise: Where is the story of the sheep and the goats found? | Matthew 25 | Genesis 1 | Exodus 20 | Psalm 23
exercise: What does a food bank do? | Gives food to families who cannot afford enough | Sells cheap food to the shops in the town for a profit | Stores money for the church in a safe | Teaches people how to grow vegetables
exercise: Which of these is a Christian charity? | Tearfund | The Football Association | The Royal Mail | The Met Office
exercise: What is tithing? | Giving a tenth of one's income to God's work | Giving half of everything you earn to the government each year | Giving a gift only at Christmas | Giving all your money away at once
exercise: If you get 10 pounds pocket money and tithe it, how much do you give? | 1 pound | 5 pounds | 10 pounds | 2 pounds
exercise: What does tithing teach about giving? | Giving can be planned, not only occasional | Giving should happen only when you feel like it | Giving is only for rich people | Giving must always be kept secret from God
exercise: What is volunteering? | Giving your time freely to help | Working for money in a shop after school | Paying someone else to do the job for you | Asking a friend for help with your homework
exercise: Which is an example of volunteering? | Visiting a lonely neighbour with a grown-up | Selling lemonade to make money for yourself | Watching TV while others tidy up | Being paid to deliver newspapers
exercise: Who do the charities like Christian Aid and CAFOD mainly help? | People in poverty and disaster | Football teams that lose matches | Only people who go to church | Rich people who want more money
test: Leo reckons charity only counts if you give money. right? | No, time and help given out of love count too | Yes, only money can really help anyone who is in need | Yes, and only big amounts count | No, charity only means giving food
test: my mate says the sheep n goats story is about farming tips. is it | No, it is about caring for people in need as caring for Jesus | Yes, Jesus was teaching how to keep animals apart | Yes, it explains why sheep are better than goats | No, it is about the first animals God made
test: A classmate who is Muslim asks why your church runs a food bank. Best answer? | Christians believe caring for the hungry is caring for Jesus | Only so the church can get more members and more money | Because the government forces every church in the country to run one | Because church food would go off otherwise
test: ugh youth group is so boring, why even go | It is a place for friends, fun and honest questions about faith | It is only for grown-ups to sing old hymns | It is where you sit tests to prove you are good enough to join the church | It is where you go to earn pocket money
test: You get 20 dollars for your birthday and want to tithe. How much would you give? | 2 dollars | 10 dollars | 20 dollars | 5 dollars
test: Your big brother says: "volunteering's for mugs, why work for nothing?" What would a Christian say? | Time given freely can be a gift as precious as money | He is right, nobody should ever help anyone without being paid | Volunteering only counts if you are an adult | Volunteering is only allowed on Sundays
test: Mia says holiday clubs at church are just babysitting. What is she missing? | They also share stories, faith and adventures | They are only for children who cannot read | They are mainly for raising money for the roof | They are only for teaching sport skills
test: Who are "the least of these" in the sheep and the goats story? | The hungry, the stranger, the sick and the prisoner | The youngest children in Sunday school | The smallest animals on the farm | The people who come last in a race
test: A friend says, "Why plan your giving? Just give when you feel like it." What does tithing teach? | That giving can be planned, not only occasional | That feelings are the only good reason to give anything | That giving is only for grown-ups who earn their own money | That planned gifts never really reach the people who need them
test: The judge asks: "Your class wants to help a food bank. What is a sensible first step?" | Ask the food bank what it needs most | Bring whatever is left in the cupboard | Bring only sweets because children like them | Give money to the first person you meet
test: Which charity is linked to the Catholic Church in England and Wales? | CAFOD | Tearfund | The Salvation Army | Oxfam
exam: Identify the principal teaching conveyed by the parable of the sheep and the goats. | That service to persons in need constitutes service to Christ | That the keeping of livestock is pleasing to God | That sheep are of greater value than goats | That the stranger ought to be turned away
exam: Determine the proportion of income traditionally surrendered under the practice of tithing. | One tenth | One half | One quarter | The entirety
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning food banks. | They furnish food to households unable to afford sufficient | They sell provisions at reduced prices for profit | They store grain for the use of the clergy | They distribute food solely to church members
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises volunteering? | The free bestowal of one's time in service of others | The performance of labour in return for wages | The payment of others to perform one's duties | The solicitation of assistance from others with one's own studies
